{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"Under Rule 6c-11, an AP must be a **member or participant of an SEC-registered clearing agency** and have a **written agreement** with the ETF or one of its service providers that permits it to place creation/redemption orders. The rule does not name DTC or NSCC, nor require the agreement to be directly with the fund; the relevant clearing-agency status and operational arrangements depend on how the orders and securities settle.\n\nThat is broader than the parties shown in particular bank-reviewed U.S. forms. Those forms may be drafted for a specific arrangement—typically the fund/Trust (or its distributor/other service provider) and the bank acting as AP—and may assume specified clearing and settlement infrastructure. Their listed parties and mechanics describe those forms, **not additional Rule 6c-11 eligibility requirements**. Conversely, a form cannot make an entity an eligible AP if it lacks the rule’s clearing-agency status."}}
